2. What I found while tracing this, beyond the finding's own description
------------------------------------------------------------------------
I read every one of the 16 frontend files that touch the `publishers`
table (not just the one named in the finding, BusinessHomeSummary.tsx)
before writing anything, to classify each as own-row, admin, or genuine
public/marketplace browsing:

  Public browsing (needed the fix): src/hooks/usePublishers.ts (the
  central data source behind 14 separate consumer pages — Browse, Home,
  PublisherProfile, ComparePublishers, MediaKit, MapView, the
  marketing-suite tools, AudienceFinder, BuildMyCampaign, CaseStudies,
  SavedLists, SavedSearches, Suburbs, Categories),
  src/components/BusinessHomeSummary.tsx (3 separate query call sites),
  src/lib/recentlyViewed.ts, src/pages/ReachChecker.tsx,
  src/pages/About.tsx, src/pages/Press.tsx (the last two are
  count-only, head:true queries for the public publisher-count stat).

  Own-row (no change needed — already gated by user_id = auth.uid()):
  src/components/PortfolioManager.tsx, src/components/
  PublisherDashboardView.tsx, src/lib/accountExport.ts, src/lib/
  campaignWorkspace.ts, src/pages/CampaignCompliance.tsx, src/pages/
  EarningsDashboard.tsx, src/pages/OpportunityFeed.tsx, src/pages/
  PublisherApply.tsx (insert only, not select).

  Admin (no change needed — already gated by is_admin()):
  src/pages/Admin.tsx, src/pages/AdminCampaigns.tsx, src/components/
  CreateRequestForClient.tsx.

I also traced every OTHER table's RLS policies that reference
`public.publishers` in a subquery (channel_requests, and similar
patterns elsewhere) to confirm none of them depend on the "approved"
branch specifically — they all match via `p.user_id = auth.uid()`, the
own-branch, so narrowing the approved branch away doesn't affect them.

Most importantly, I found a real, non-obvious dependency that the
finding didn't mention: calculate_trust_score(), calculate_publisher_
score(), assign_publisher_level(), and refresh_publisher_scores() (all
in schema_phase5.sql, later redefined by schema_phase9.sql and
schema_phase11.sql) were never declared SECURITY DEFINER. refresh_
publisher_scores() is called by a trigger that fires `after insert or
update on public.reviews`, and reviews_insert_own_completed
(schema_phase2.sql) lets an ordinary BUSINESS — not the publisher, not
an admin — insert a review after their own completed request. Because
these functions default to SECURITY INVOKER, that trigger's internal
`select * from publishers where id = ...` runs under the REVIEWING
BUSINESS's own RLS. Today that succeeds because of the very "approved"
branch this fix needed to remove. If I'd removed that branch without
also fixing these functions, every review submission would have started
silently zeroing out the reviewed publisher's trust_score and
publisher_score, because the SELECT would return no row and the
calculation functions fall back to returning 0 when nothing is found.
This would have been a severe, silent regression shipped alongside a
security fix — found by tracing the actual trigger chain, not assumed.

