3. Files changed
------------------------------------------------------------------------
- supabase/schema_phase82_restrict_public_publisher_columns.sql (NEW)
    - Redefines calculate_trust_score(), calculate_publisher_score(),
      assign_publisher_level(), and refresh_publisher_scores() as
      SECURITY DEFINER (with set search_path = public). Every function
      body is copied verbatim from its latest existing definition
      (schema_phase11.sql for calculate_trust_score, schema_phase9.sql
      for refresh_publisher_scores, schema_phase5.sql for the other
      two) — this is purely a security-context annotation change, no
      logic was touched.
    - Creates public.publishers_public, a view exposing only the columns
      the public-facing marketplace UI actually reads (checked directly
      against every usePublishers() consumer, plus
      MarketplaceProfileView.tsx for channel_metadata specifically) for
      status = 'approved' rows. Explicitly excludes: email,
      mobile_number, business_name, company_registration, vat_number,
      admin_notes, rejected_reason, reviewed_at, payout_method,
      payout_details, payout_account_verified_at, authenticity_risk,
      authenticity_notes, authenticity_checked_at, email_verified,
      phone_verified, identity_verified, account_age_months, and
      posting_frequency. Documented in a `comment on view` so this stays
      visible from inside the database itself, not just this file.
      Relies on the standard Postgres/Supabase "safe view" pattern:
      created via the SQL editor, so it's owned by the `postgres` role,
      and an ordinary view without security_invoker = true (the
      Postgres default) resolves the underlying table access using the
      VIEW OWNER's privileges — postgres bypasses RLS entirely — so the
      view's own `where status = 'approved'` becomes the only row filter
      that applies, for anon and authenticated alike.
    - Grants SELECT on publishers_public to anon and authenticated.
    - Drops publishers_select_approved_or_own_or_admin and replaces it
      with publishers_select_own_or_admin — the "status = 'approved'"
      branch is removed from the base table entirely. Public browsing
      now exclusively goes through publishers_public; a logged-in
      publisher still sees their own full row, and admins still see
      every column of every row.

