4. Why this shape, not a different one
------------------------------------------------------------------------
- A safe view, not a second "public_publishers" table or an RPC, because
  the finding's own required-fix list offered "safe view/RPC or split
  into separate tables" as options, and a view is the lowest-risk of the
  three here: no data migration, no dual-write risk between two tables
  staying in sync, and it reads naturally through the same PostgREST/
  supabase-js `.from()` interface the frontend already uses everywhere
  else, so the touched files only needed a one-line table-name swap.
- Kept the view's column list to what's actually rendered today (traced
  against real consumer code) rather than "everything that isn't on the
  finding's never-expose list" — a narrower default surface is safer,
  and any field a future feature genuinely needs to show publicly can be
  added to the view explicitly, as a deliberate decision, rather than
  already being exposed by default.
- Made the four scoring functions SECURITY DEFINER instead of leaving
  the approved branch in place for their sake, because that would have
  meant not actually fixing the finding — the whole point is that
  "approved" should no longer grant column-unrestricted access to
  anyone, and these functions don't need a business's row-visibility at
  all; they're server-computed fields (trust_score, publisher_score,
  level) no ordinary user can write to directly in the first place, so
  running them as definer is the more correct design regardless of this
  fix, not just a workaround for it.
- Fixed the one pre-existing test assertion this change would have
  broken, rather than leaving a known-stale test in the suite — a pgTAP
  file whose own comments claim to test current behavior but assert the
  pre-fix behavior would be actively misleading to whoever runs this
  suite for the first time.

